window.manualIndex = [{"url": "index.html", "title": "Overview", "chapter": "Overview", "group": "Getting started", "top": true, "body": "An illustrated player's guide to the Teensy 4.1 MIDI looper."}, {"url": "index.html#quick-guides", "title": "Quick guides", "chapter": "Overview", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "Performance Overdub & undo ↗ Overdub a second part, undo the take and bring it back. Set library Save & recall ↗ Save a version of your set or recall one of its loops. Control surface Map a controller ↗ Map MIDI notes, faders and LED feedback on your controller."}, {"url": "index.html#instrument-goal", "title": "Keep playing, keep the vibe", "chapter": "Overview", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "The inspiration was Cubase on an Atari. You sat down, the ideas went straight in, and the machine seemed to disappear. That’s the feeling behind MidiLooper. “One hand on the device, attention on the music.” When a loop comes round and you can already hear the next part in your head, hit Record and play it. If the loop felt better before the overdub, undo it while you listen. Bring it back if you miss it. The dream is to catch a whole jam that way and shape it into a song while you can still feel what made it work. Each track plays one of its eight loop slots. A slot keeps its loop and undo history. A set holds all eight tracks. Save a milestone when you hear a version of the set you want to come back to."}, {"url": "index.html#hardware-requirements", "title": "Hardware requirements", "chapter": "Overview", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "The reference instrument uses a Teensy 4.1, 8 MB of external PSRAM, a 256 × 64 SSD1322 OLED and a microSD card for saving. A push encoder adjusts notes and groove settings. A MIDI controller supplies the recording and transport buttons. The DROID setup adds a grid and four motorized faders. MIDI circuit schematic → Display, encoder and button wiring → From the device Read the OLED Find the selected slot, read the piano roll and recognize a completed save. Open the screen guide →"}, {"url": "setup.html", "title": "Hardware & setup", "chapter": "Hardware & setup", "group": "Getting started", "top": true, "body": "Connect a keyboard, controller and sound source. Check the MIDI route and prepare the card before your first take."}, {"url": "setup.html#hardware-requirements", "title": "Hardware requirements", "chapter": "Hardware & setup", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "Part What to fit or connect Teensy and memory Teensy 4.1 with 8 MB of external PSRAM. If fitting a single PSRAM chip, use the smaller QSPI footprint on the underside of the board. Teensy 4.1 memory and pinout Display 256 × 64 OLED with the SSD1322 controller, set up for four-wire SPI. It shows the piano roll, track and slot, transport, tempo and save status. Storage Use the onboard microSD socket for autosave and your set library. Allow about 2 GiB of free space for the two files together. Insert the card before powering up. Controls The base module has a push encoder and four momentary buttons. The encoder edits notes and groove settings. For record, overdub and transport on this firmware, use a MIDI controller. The reference DROID setup has a button grid and four motorized faders. MIDI connections A USB data cable connects the Teensy to a computer. A USB MIDI controller connects to the separate five-pin USB host header using a host cable. For five-pin DIN instruments, fit the MIDI IN and OUT circuits below."}, {"url": "setup.html#connect-the-instrument", "title": "Connect a keyboard, controller and synth", "chapter": "Hardware & setup", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "Power up. The firmware covered by this manual displays OSTINATIX v0.7 at startup. Connect the MIDI input and output. Connect your keyboard’s MIDI OUT to the looper’s DIN IN, or plug a compatible USB MIDI keyboard into the Teensy’s USB host port. A computer can also send MIDI to the Teensy over USB. Connect the looper’s DIN OUT to your synth’s MIDI IN. For a software instrument, route the Teensy’s USB MIDI output to an instrument track in your DAW. Set the MIDI channels. Start with your keyboard transmitting on channel 1 and your synth receiving on channel 1. Use a separate controller or a pad bank on channel 16 for looper controls. The DROID patch maps the controls for you. On another controller, set the pads to send a MIDI note when pressed and release it when lifted. Check MIDI thru. Select track 1 and play a key. You should hear the receiving instrument through its audio output. The looper sends playback notes to DIN OUT and to the computer over USB. The Teensy’s USB host port sends MIDI feedback for controller LEDs and motorized faders. Keyboard Transmit · Ch 1 → MidiLooper Track 1 → Synth Receive · Ch 1 Map the pads and faders or download the DROID patch →"}, {"url": "setup.html#din-midi", "title": "Wire the five-pin MIDI input and output", "chapter": "Hardware & setup", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "The looper’s DIN IN and OUT use Serial8 at 31,250 baud. For one pair of five-pin DIN sockets, use a 6N138 optocoupler, a 1N4148 diode, a 0.1 µF bypass capacitor and the resistors listed below. Identify the numbered terminals on each socket before soldering. Its solder side is the mirror image of its mating face. Connection Wire to DIN IN, pin 4 Through 220 Ω to the 6N138 input, pin 2. DIN IN, pin 5 Directly to 6N138 pin 3. Connect the 1N4148 across pins 2 and 3, with the banded end (cathode) on pin 2. Leave DIN IN pins 1, 2 and 3 open to preserve isolation. 6N138 power Pin 8 to 5 V and pin 5 to Teensy GND. Put the 0.1 µF capacitor across pins 8 and 5. Leave 6N138 pins 1, 4 and 7 open. Teensy MIDI receive Connect 6N138 pin 6 directly to Teensy RX8 (pin 34). Add a 470 Ω pull-up from that junction to 3.3 V. DIN OUT, pins 4 and 5 Connect 3.3 V through 47 Ω to DIN OUT pin 4. Connect Teensy TX8 (pin 35) through a second 47 Ω resistor to DIN OUT pin 5. DIN OUT, pin 2 Connect to Teensy GND. Leave DIN OUT pins 1 and 3 open. These connections follow PJRC’s Teensy 4.x MIDI circuit with the looper’s RX8 and TX8 pins. Original circuit schematic · MIDI wiring and DIN connector pinout . Optional eight-port setup. With an external 8-in/8-out MIDI interface on a computer, your DAW can route the interface inputs to the looper over USB and send the looper’s eight playback channels to individual interface outputs. A standalone Teensy expansion would need separate DIN circuits, pin assignments and firmware routing for the additional serial ports. The current build drives one onboard DIN pair. Firmware port setup ."}, {"url": "setup.html#hardware", "title": "Connect the display, encoder and buttons", "chapter": "Hardware & setup", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "Base module. The encoder edits notes and groove settings. The current firmware reads the transport and recording buttons from an external MIDI controller. DROID controller. The button grid selects tracks, slots and steps. The four faders adjust notes, loop boundaries, tempo or groove, depending on the mode. Control or signal Teensy 4.1 connection OLED data and clock MOSI (display data in) → pin 11. SCK (serial clock) → pin 13. OLED control lines Chip select (CS) → 10. Data/command (DC) → 9. Reset → 8. Use the supply voltage specified for your display module and connect its ground to the Teensy’s ground. Rotary encoder knob Quadrature outputs A → 29 and B → 30. Connect its common contact to ground. Connect the push switch between pin 31 and ground. The firmware uses the internal pull-up. Base-module buttons The reference panel wires A, B, C and D to pins 36, 37, 38 and 39 respectively. Their performance actions are currently available over MIDI. Base-module pin assignments . External knobs and faders The DROID faders send MIDI. A different controller can use knobs or faders that send the same CC or Pitch Bend messages. Connect these through the controller’s MIDI port. Fader assignments . Check the pins against the firmware and your board while wiring. OLED signal pins · Encoder wiring · Teensy pinout cards ."}, {"url": "setup.html#clock", "title": "Choose the timing source", "chapter": "Hardware & setup", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "For a first take, turn off the DAW or sequencer’s MIDI Clock output. Press PLAY/STOP to start playback with the looper’s internal clock. In LOOP_EDIT, F4 sets the tempo in whole beats per minute and F3 adjusts it in tenths. The looper uses 192 ticks per quarter note. To sync with a DAW or sequencer, enable MIDI Clock on its output to the looper. MIDI Clock sends 24 pulses per quarter note. The looper divides each pulse interval into eight internal ticks to follow the incoming tempo. MIDI Start, Continue and Stop control the transport. The clock icon brightens and shows an incoming arrow. Some sequencers keep sending clock pulses while stopped. In that case, tap the looper’s global PLAY/STOP to start on the next pulse. If the sequencer is already playing, the same button stops the looper’s tracks locally. Press it again to rejoin at the next bar. Set the tempo on the device sending MIDI Clock."}, {"url": "setup.html#first-save", "title": "Prepare the card for your first take", "chapter": "Hardware & setup", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "Insert the microSD card before powering up. A new card starts in read-only mode, shown as RO . With the transport stopped and recording finished, open the Teensy’s USB serial monitor at 115200 baud. Type this command and press Return. !ENGINE approve-saving The looper remembers this setting on the card. After recording, the four save dots flash bright when the changes have been written. See Save & load sets to keep a named stage of the arrangement."}, {"url": "setup.html#firmware", "title": "Identify the firmware build", "chapter": "Hardware & setup", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "To compile the firmware described here, run pio run -e teensy41-engine from the repository root. The separate teensy41-capture-serial environment builds the legacy looper, which has different controls and a different save format. A CR2032 on VBAT and GND keeps the clock running between sessions so saved sets keep their dates. If the display says TIME NOT SET , check the clock with !ENGINE time ."}, {"url": "first-recording.html", "title": "Record your first loop", "chapter": "Your first loop", "group": "Getting started", "top": true, "body": "Record a loop into an empty slot, finish the take on a downbeat and listen to it repeat."}, {"url": "first-recording.html#record-the-first-phrase", "title": "Record a one-bar loop", "chapter": "Your first loop", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "For this one-bar bass line, use the looper’s internal clock. Connect a keyboard or sequencer sending notes on MIDI channel 1 and a synth or software instrument receiving on channel 1. Use a controller with buttons assigned to the looper’s track, slot and transport controls. The looper records the MIDI performance. Your receiving instrument supplies the sound. Arm a slot for recording. From the performance screen, tap track 1, then tap an empty slot on that track. Check TRACK and SLOT on the OLED. A pulsing record circle means the slot is record-armed. Start the transport. Tap global PLAY/STOP (MIDI note number 39 on channel 16). Recording begins on the downbeat. The circle becomes solid and the playhead moves. With internal clock, you can also start by tapping the armed slot again. Play the bass line. Count four beats as you play. The piano roll shows each note and BARS shows the length of the take in bars. Finish the take. Tap REC as the next downbeat arrives. Your loop plays back from the first beat. Let it repeat once. When you are ready to add notes, another tap of REC starts an overdub. Firmware-rendered example. The solid record dot and the BARS counter show a take in progress. The firmware drew this screen from sample notes. Starting from the slot button? Wait for the pulsing record circle before you tap the slot a second time. Two quick taps count as a double-tap."}, {"url": "first-recording.html#which-bar", "title": "Which bar ends the take?", "chapter": "Your first loop", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "For a one-bar take, aim for beat 1 of bar 2. If you tap REC up to two beats after that downbeat, the loop can still end at the bar line you just passed. Later in the bar, the looper waits for the next downbeat. The same half-bar allowance applies after each complete bar."}, {"url": "first-recording.html#record-while-running", "title": "Record while other parts play", "chapter": "Your first loop", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "While the transport runs, select another track and tap an empty slot. If that track is stopped, the armed take starts on the next downbeat. If that track is playing a loop, the take starts when its loop repeats. To punch in sooner on the playing track, tap the armed slot a second time. The countdown above the song position shows when recording will begin. Only one track records or overdubs at a time. Finish that take before recording the next one. The other tracks continue playing."}, {"url": "first-recording.html#check-playback", "title": "If the loop looks right but sounds silent", "chapter": "Your first loop", "group": "Getting started", "top": false, "body": "If the piano roll shows notes, match the instrument’s receive channel to the track’s MIDI output channel on the OLED. Track 1 plays on channel 1, track 2 on channel 2, and so on. Check whether the track is muted or another track is soloed. If the piano roll is empty, check that your keyboard sends notes on a channel from 1 to 12. Channels 13–16 carry the looper’s controls. To keep the loop after switching off, enable card saving during setup and wait for the save to finish. RO on the OLED means the looper is still in read-only mode."}, {"url": "overdub-undo.html", "title": "Overdub & undo", "chapter": "Overdub & undo", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": true, "body": "Try a second part over a playing loop. Step back through that take or return to an earlier version of the selected slot."}, {"url": "overdub-undo.html#add-an-overdub", "title": "Overdub a part while the loop plays", "chapter": "Overdub & undo", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Select the track that is playing your recorded loop. Confirm its TRACK and SLOT numbers on the performance screen. Tap REC once. The loop keeps playing and ODUB appears on the OLED. Play a new part over it. You can add more notes each time the loop repeats. Tap REC again to finish the overdub, even halfway through a bar. You hear the new notes on each repeat. The loop stays the same length. If the track is stopped, tapping REC on a slot that contains a loop starts playback first. Tap it again while the loop plays to overdub. A long press of REC clears the selected slot."}, {"url": "overdub-undo.html#undo-and-redo", "title": "Hear the loop with and without your change", "chapter": "Overdub & undo", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Double-tap REC on the selected slot to undo its most recent change. After finishing an overdub, you’ll hear the loop as it sounded before you added the new part. Triple-tap REC to bring the overdub back. Make the three taps in quick succession. If you make a new change to the slot after undoing, its earlier redos are discarded. Read the undo count. UNDO shows how many steps you can undo on the selected slot. ODUB counts undo steps in the current overdub, and EDIT counts steps in the current note edit. During an overdub, each completed pass around the loop that adds material can be undone separately. After you finish the take, one undo removes the whole overdub. While NOTE_EDIT is open, double-tap REC to undo your last note edit, or triple-tap to redo it. After you leave the editor, one undo brings the loop back to the way it sounded before that visit."}, {"url": "overdub-undo.html#undo-target", "title": "Choose the slot you mean to change", "chapter": "Overdub & undo", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Undo follows the selected slot. For example, to undo a bass overdub recorded on track 1, slot 4, select track 1 and its fourth slot first. After copying a loop, undo the paste on the slot you copied it to. After clearing a slot, double-tap REC there to restore the loop if that undo step is still available."}, {"url": "overdub-undo.html#older-undo-steps", "title": "Reach an older step on the card", "chapter": "Overdub & undo", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Each slot keeps up to 99 versions of its loop, counting the current version and those available on the card. The looper holds 99 versions in memory for all tracks combined. With card saving enabled, it can recall older versions from the card as well. The UNDO count includes the steps you can reach in both places. When a slot reaches its limit, its oldest version gives way to the next change. Double-tap REC as usual. If the looper needs an older take from the card, the current loop keeps playing until that version is ready. If you ask for several more undo steps while it loads, playback switches once to the last version you chose. If memory fills before the card has saved a change, that undo step can be lost. If the looper cannot read the older version or has too little memory to play it, the current loop keeps playing. Clearing the slot or loading a set cancels the recall. Which undo steps come back? Reopening the autosaved working set after power-up restores recent versions and older steps saved on the card. Loading a milestone restores the arrangement and whichever steps were in memory when you saved it. A milestone leaves out versions that were already held only on the card."}, {"url": "switching.html", "title": "Switching loops", "chapter": "Switching loops", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": true, "body": "Switch to a variation at the end of a loop, or launch it on the next sixteenth note while the other tracks play."}, {"url": "switching.html#tracks-and-slots", "title": "Choose where the next part will play", "chapter": "Switching loops", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Each of the eight tracks has eight loop slots and plays one slot at a time. Put a verse bass line in slot 1 and a chorus bass line in slot 2 of the same track to switch between them. Put the chords on another track to hear the chords and bass together. Tap the bass track’s button in track select mode, or tap TRK to move to the next track. Check the TRACK number on the OLED. While the verse plays, tap the chorus slot. Its button flashes and its notes appear on the piano roll. The countdown shows how much of the verse remains. The chorus begins when the verse loop ends. To bring the chorus in sooner, double-tap its slot button. It starts on the next division of the launch grid. The chords on the other track carry on. Firmware-rendered example. The play triangle shows that this sample two-bar part is running. Check TRACK and SLOT here before choosing another part. With the global transport stopped, tap a slot containing a loop to select it for the next start. With the clock running and that track stopped, the same tap starts its loop on the launch grid. Tapping the currently playing slot mutes or unmutes its track if no other slot is waiting to play. Tapping an empty slot arms it for recording."}, {"url": "switching.html#launch-grid", "title": "Set the launch grid", "chapter": "Switching loops", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "The launch grid sets when a queued loop can start. By default, a double-tapped slot starts on the next sixteenth note. To choose a grid for all tracks, send one of these commands over USB serial. !ENGINE launch-q 16 selects sixteenth notes. !ENGINE launch-q 8 selects eighth notes. !ENGINE launch-q 4 selects quarter notes. !ENGINE launch-q bar selects the downbeat of each bar. The looper remembers the setting after power-off. A single tap from a playing loop still waits for loop end. To jump within a playing loop, tap one of the 16 step buttons for a sixteenth-note position in the current bar. The eight bar buttons jump to bars in the current group of eight. The jump takes effect on the next grid division. If you shortened the loop, expand its range before jumping to a recorded bar outside it."}, {"url": "switching.html#mute-solo-stop", "title": "Mute, solo or stop", "chapter": "Switching loops", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Mute. Double-tap a track button to mute or unmute it. To mute several parts, tap EDIT once. The eight track buttons now mute and unmute with one tap each. A bright track LED means it contains a loop and is unmuted, a dim LED means it contains a loop and is muted, and a dark LED means it is empty. Tap EDIT again to select tracks. Solo. Hold a track button for about 0.6 seconds and release. Hold it again to cancel its solo. More than one track can be soloed. Stop one track. Tap the track play/stop control (MIDI note number 40 on channel 16). Tap again to start its selected loop on the launch grid. Stop all tracks. Tap global transport (MIDI note number 39 on channel 16). With internal clock, the next start plays the selected loop on each unmuted track and starts an armed take. When an external sequencer supplies MIDI Clock, global transport stops the looper’s tracks while the sequencer keeps running. Tap it again to rejoin on the next downbeat. If the external transport has stopped, starting the looper locally uses the next incoming clock pulse as the downbeat."}, {"url": "switching.html#copy-a-variation", "title": "Make a variation and keep the original", "chapter": "Switching loops", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Hold the slot button for the loop you want to copy for 0.6 seconds. Its LED pulses and the COPY window opens. Release the button. Choose another track if needed, then tap the slot where the copy should go. Choose an empty slot for a new variation. If the slot already holds a loop, the copy replaces it. Its LED flashes three times. Edit or overdub the copied loop to make a variation. To undo the paste, double-tap REC on the slot you copied to. To cancel before pasting, tap the original slot once or wait five seconds."}, {"url": "editing.html", "title": "Edit notes & loop ranges", "chapter": "Notes & loop ranges", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": true, "body": "Move a note, adjust its velocity or repeat a few bars from a longer loop. The original bars stay available inside the slot."}, {"url": "editing.html#choose-an-edit-mode", "title": "Open note or loop editing", "chapter": "Notes & loop ranges", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Select a slot that contains a loop. Hold EDIT for about 0.6 seconds and release before 1.5 seconds. In NOTE_EDIT the piano roll highlights the selected note. Hold and release again for LOOP_EDIT , where the bottom row shows loop start, loop length and tempo. An empty slot opens directly in LOOP_EDIT. To return to the performance screen, hold EDIT for 1.5 seconds. If the track buttons start muting tracks, you gave EDIT a short tap. Tap once to restore track selection, then hold and release to enter the editor."}, {"url": "editing.html#edit-one-note", "title": "Correct a note while listening", "chapter": "Notes & loop ranges", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Select the note. The dotted bracket marks your position on the piano roll. Tap the step button for the note’s sixteenth-note position in that bar. If several notes start on the same step, tap it again to select the next one. F1 also selects notes. Adjust pitch and timing. F4 changes pitch. To move the note’s start, use F2 to choose the sixteenth-note position and F3 to nudge it earlier or later. To shorten or lengthen it, tap NOTELEN with the note selected. F2 and F3 now move the end of the note. Tap NOTELEN again to edit its start. Set velocity. Hold NOTELEN and move F4 to change the note’s MIDI velocity. A velocity of zero mutes the note. Raise it again to hear the note play. Keep the edit. While editing, double-tap REC to undo or triple-tap to redo. When the loop sounds right, hold EDIT for 1.5 seconds. All the note edits made during that visit now count as one undo step on this slot. Firmware-rendered example. The note starts on beat 3 of bar 1. LENGTH gives its duration in ticks, with 192 ticks per quarter note. To use the rotary encoder, push it to choose pitch, start position, duration or velocity. Turn it to change the underlined value. Holding a step button while turning the encoder edits the note on that step."}, {"url": "editing.html#add-delete-copy", "title": "Add, delete or copy notes", "chapter": "Notes & loop ranges", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Tap an empty step to add a sixteenth note at MIDI note number 60, velocity 80. The OLED calls MIDI note 60 C3 . At an empty bracket, tapping NOTELEN adds a thirty-second note. Double-tap a step that contains notes to delete the selected note on that step. To copy a bar, hold its bar button until COPY opens, then release. Tap the destination bar once to replace its notes, or double-tap to add the copied notes to the notes already there. To copy one note, hold its step button without editing, release when COPY opens and choose a destination step. Pasting beyond the loop end extends the loop to the end of that bar. Tap the original bar or step once to cancel copying, or double-tap it to clear it."}, {"url": "editing.html#set-the-play-range", "title": "Set the loop start and end", "chapter": "Notes & loop ranges", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Enter LOOP_EDIT on a recorded loop. To hear bars 2–4, hold bar button 2 and tap bar button 4. The range includes both ends. Holding 4 and tapping 2 selects the same range. Choose another range, such as bars 1–2. The bar numbers still refer to your original recording. Tap NOTELEN to alternate between the two ranges. Double-tap it to play the full loop. To loop a single bar, double-tap its bar button. To repeat a single sixteenth note’s worth of time, double-tap its step button. Hold one step and tap another to repeat everything between them. The brackets around the progress bar mark the range. Notes outside it are still there when you lengthen the loop. Firmware-rendered example. START, LENGTH and BPM identify LOOP_EDIT. This sample loop starts on the first beat and runs for two bars. F1 moves the loop start in sixteenth-note steps. With F2, choose a loop length and let the fader rest. The shortest settings are a sixteenth note, an eighth note, a quarter note, half a bar and three beats. Longer settings are 1–16 bars, then 32, 64, 96 and 128 bars. F4 sets the internal tempo from 20 to 300 BPM in whole beats per minute. F3 adjusts it in tenths of a BPM. With external MIDI Clock, set the tempo on the device sending clock."}, {"url": "editing.html#start-with-a-length", "title": "Start with a fixed-length loop", "chapter": "Notes & loop ranges", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "With an empty slot selected in LOOP_EDIT, tap NOTELEN to create an empty four-bar loop. To choose another length, hold and release NOTELEN . Tap it once for the next choice or twice for the previous one. F2 also sets the length. Choose FULL, saved ranges A or B, 1–8 bars, or 16, 32, 64 or 128 bars. Stop adjusting for three seconds to keep the length. Hold NOTELEN again to cancel the changes made in this window. Hold EDIT for 1.5 seconds to leave. Double-tap the slot button to launch the loop. When the playhead moves, tap REC to overdub. Each pass now repeats at the length you chose."}, {"url": "groove.html", "title": "Groove & quantize", "chapter": "Groove & quantize", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": true, "body": "Compare two groove templates with your original timing. Adjust the amount of swing or quantization while the loop plays."}, {"url": "groove.html#open-groove", "title": "Open GROOVE on a recorded loop", "chapter": "Groove & quantize", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Start playback on the loop you want to adjust. On the supplied DROID patch, hold PLAY/STOP to open GROOVE for that slot. On a custom controller, assign a button to MIDI note number 43 on channel 16. From the performance screen you can also hold NOTELEN (note 35) and press global transport (note 39), both on channel 16. Read OFF , A and B across the top. The highlight shows which timing you hear. OFF plays the loop with its original timing. A starts with the loop’s current groove. B starts with the same settings, ready for comparison. A fresh recording opens on OFF, with straight sixteenth-note quantization at 50% strength available in both A and B. Firmware-rendered example. A plays a sixteenth-note swing. B has a later offbeat and a higher amount. Tap ADD to hear how the second setting shifts the offbeats."}, {"url": "groove.html#audition-a-feel", "title": "Compare two grooves", "chapter": "Groove & quantize", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Choose the groove. While A or OFF plays, use F3 to choose B’s groove and F4 for its strength. Try straight sixteenth notes in A and sixteenth-note swing in B. Swing delays the second sixteenth in each pair. You can set up B while listening to the loop with A or OFF selected. Compare by ear. Tap ADD (the NOTELEN button) to alternate A and B. From OFF, the first tap selects A. Double-tap to return to OFF and hear the timing you played. Adjust the strength. F1 chooses the groove for A and F2 sets its AMOUNT. F3 and F4 do the same for B. At 100%, each affected note starts on the nearest timing position in that groove. At 50%, it moves halfway from the start you played. To use the encoder, tap REC to move up or EDIT to move down, then turn to change the highlighted setting. Keep the timing you hear. Choose OFF, A or B and hold PLAY/STOP to close the window. To undo the groove changes you made during this visit, double-tap REC back on the performance screen. A groove affects the whole loop. You can restore your recorded timing with OFF, and notes moved by hand in NOTE_EDIT keep their positions. After you release a fader, listen for the change on a beat. The dots stay dim while you adjust, move while the looper prepares the new timing, then briefly brighten when it is playing."}, {"url": "groove.html#cycle-and-auto-quantize", "title": "Compare hands-free and include new overdubs", "chapter": "Groove & quantize", "group": "Playing & editing", "top": false, "body": "Hold ADD to alternate two bars of A with two bars of B. The bar under the playing groove fills as the next change approaches. Tap ADD once to stay on the groove you hear, or double-tap to hear the original recording. Tap TRK in GROOVE to switch AUTO Q on or off for this slot. With AUTO Q on and a groove selected, newly overdubbed notes take on that groove when you finish the overdub. Leave AUTO Q off to preserve the timing you play in new overdubs. Which groove am I hearing? Check the highlight over OFF, A or B. Tap ADD to compare, or hold PLAY/STOP to leave the window while the loop keeps playing."}, {"url": "library.html", "title": "Save & load sets", "chapter": "Save & load sets", "group": "Library & controls", "top": true, "body": "Autosave keeps the current set on the card. Save a milestone to recall all eight tracks together or load a single loop from an earlier take."}, {"url": "library.html#autosave", "title": "Keep the set you’re playing", "chapter": "Save & load sets", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "Your current set holds eight MIDI tracks, each with eight loop slots. Autosave remembers your loops and undo history between sessions. To keep a version you can load after you’ve played more takes, save a milestone of the whole set. Put a microSD card in the Teensy’s built-in socket before powering up. Leave the transport stopped. Connect the Teensy to your computer over USB and open its serial monitor at 115200 baud. Send !ENGINE approve-saving and press Enter. The looper remembers this choice on the card. RO means new recordings stay in memory. You’ll see it on a fresh card until you enable saving, or if the card was missing or unreadable at power-up. Finish your take or leave NOTE_EDIT. Watch the four dots above the tempo. Dim dots mean a save is waiting. A moving dot means the looper is writing to the card. Four bright dots flash when the save is complete. A dimmer flash signals a failed save. In the sets browser, the save row reads UNSAVED or SAVED , with the time if the clock is set. At power-up the looper restores the last saved version of each slot from /MidiLooper/engine/store.bin . This file takes about 1 GiB and holds recent and older undo steps. Saved milestones and the recovery checkpoint share a second 1 GiB file, /MidiLooper/library/library.bin . Allow room for both and leave the card in place until the save finishes. FROM B01 tells you the current set started from Building 01. With SAVE SET highlighted, press TRK to save the version you’re playing now. Firmware-rendered example."}, {"url": "library.html#save-a-milestone", "title": "Save a version of the whole set", "chapter": "Save & load sets", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "The sets browser is available once the looper has opened the card’s library. If holding Next Track mutes the selected track instead, hold it again to restore the previous mute setting. Check the card and saving setup above. The USB serial command !ENGINE library reports whether the library is mounted. From the performance screen, hold Next Track . The SETS window opens with SAVE SET highlighted. Press TRK (the same Next Track button). In SAVE CURRENT , press REC to move up or EDIT to move down. Label this version as SKETCH , BUILDING , EXPLORATION , TAKE , ARRANGEMENT , or FINAL . Choose AS NEW SET to take the same loops in a different direction under a new set number. Press TRK once. For example, SAVED SET 2 T01 confirms the first Take in set 2. Saving another Take makes T02. You can still load T01. Press TRK to save Building B02. The earlier Building B01 and Sketch S01 stay in set 1. Firmware-rendered example. Each milestone keeps all 64 slots, the selected track and slots, the tempo and the undo steps still in memory when you press Save. Older undo steps held only on the card are outside both milestones and recovery checkpoints. If you overdub again after saving, that performance becomes part of the current set. Hold a button to leave the browser."}, {"url": "library.html#load-a-set", "title": "Load a saved set", "chapter": "Save & load sets", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "Finish recording or overdubbing and leave NOTE_EDIT. Hold Next Track to open SETS . Under LOAD SET , use EDIT to move down to the set you want. Press TRK to see its saved stages, newest first. Under LOAD STAGE , check the stage number and date, then press TRK . The performance screen flashes LOADED SET with the set and stage you chose. Sketch S01 is highlighted. Press TRK once to load that version of set 1. Firmware-rendered example. Loading a set replaces the loops and undo history on all eight tracks. Before switching, the looper saves one recovery checkpoint so you can return to the set you were playing. Send !ENGINE return in the USB serial monitor to recall it. The next set you load replaces that checkpoint. To keep a version for future sessions, save it as a milestone. If the transport is running, the looper prepares the incoming loops and switches in time. Send !ENGINE switch-at bar to change sets on the next bar line, or !ENGINE switch-at loop to wait for the end of the selected track’s loop. If an incoming track needs more time to load, you may hear a gap of about one bar on that track."}, {"url": "library.html#get-a-loop", "title": "Load one loop into a track and slot", "chapter": "Save & load sets", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "In your current set, select the track and slot where you want the loop to play. Open SETS , enter the set that holds the loop, choose GET A LOOP , then choose its saved stage. The rows are tracks. The columns are loop slots. NOTELEN moves up. The selected-track Play/Stop control (MIDI note 40 on channel 16) moves down. REC moves left and EDIT moves right. Pause on a filled slot to see its piano roll. Press TRK to AUDITION the loop. With playback stopped or the track muted, you can still see the preview. Move to another slot to end the audition. When the hint changes to USE , press TRK again. COPIED TO 2-1 , for example, means the loop is now on track 2, slot 1. To go back, undo on that slot. You can still recall the original loop from the saved stage. On this screen, PLY means note 40. The supplied DROID patch sends note 39 from its global Play/Stop button. To move down through the tracks, assign a spare pad to note 40 on channel 16, with Note On when pressed and Note Off when released. The extra column on the left offers empty loops of different lengths. Choose a length and press TRK to load it. The looper asks for confirmation if the chosen track and slot already hold a loop. INTO 2-1 means track 2, slot 1 will receive the loop. The highlighted 4 selects an empty four-bar loop. The two lit slots to its right contain loops from Building B01. Firmware-rendered example."}, {"url": "library.html#trash-and-restore", "title": "Trash and restore saved stages", "chapter": "Save & load sets", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "In a set’s stage list, double-press TRK on a milestone to move it to the trash immediately. On MOVE SET TO TRASH the same gesture moves every visible stage of that set. Watch for IN TRASH . Triple-press TRK immediately afterward to bring back the last stage or set you trashed. To recover an earlier stage or set, open TRASH from the main sets list, highlight it and press TRK . The screen flashes RESTORED with the set or stage number. Confirming EMPTY TRASH permanently removes your ability to recall those stages. The library file still occupies the same space on the card, and its used space stays allocated."}, {"url": "library.html#storage-messages", "title": "If a set won’t save or load", "chapter": "Save & load sets", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "STOP THE TAKE FIRST or CLOSE THE EDIT FIRST means finish recording, overdubbing or note editing, then try again. BUSY means the looper is still using the card. Wait for it to finish. For LIBRARY NOT READY , check the card and first-time saving setup. LIBRARY FULL means there’s no room for this milestone. The looper checks the available space before saving. CHECKPOINT NOT WRITTEN means the current set is still loaded. If the looper cannot read the selected milestone, it tries to bring back your previous set from the checkpoint. SET NOT RESTORED means that also failed. Keep the card and check which saved stages you can still load before recording again. How the looper stores undo history · Sets browser controls · Save and load in the firmware"}, {"url": "midi-controls.html", "title": "Map your MIDI controller", "chapter": "MIDI control map", "group": "Library & controls", "top": true, "body": "Assign MIDI notes to pads and Control Change or Pitch Bend messages to faders. Use MIDI feedback from the looper to drive the controller’s LEDs and motorized faders."}, {"url": "midi-controls.html#wire-a-controller", "title": "Connect a controller and a sound source", "chapter": "MIDI control map", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "The looper records MIDI notes and other MIDI messages. The receiving instrument produces the sound. Connect the looper’s DIN MIDI OUT to a synthesizer’s MIDI IN, or route the Teensy’s USB MIDI output to a software instrument. Listen through the synthesizer’s audio output or your computer’s audio interface. For a first test, select track 1 and set the instrument to receive on MIDI channel 1. Computer or DAW. Connect the Teensy’s USB device port to your computer. In your DAW, choose the Teensy as the MIDI destination for keyboard notes, sequencer tracks or control-pad messages. To play a software instrument from the looper, select the Teensy as that instrument track’s MIDI input. Standalone controller. Plug a compatible USB MIDI controller into the Teensy’s USB host port. For a controller with 5-pin DIN connectors, connect its MIDI OUT to the looper’s MIDI IN. To receive LED or motorized-fader feedback over DIN, also route the looper’s MIDI OUT to the controller’s MIDI IN. MIDI inputs and outputs. The looper receives MIDI through DIN IN, USB from a computer or the Teensy’s USB host port. It sends loop playback and live MIDI thru to DIN OUT and back to the computer over USB. The USB host port receives notes and controls from a connected MIDI controller and sends LED and motorized-fader feedback back to it. MIDI channels. Play on any of channels 1–12. Channels 13–16 are reserved for looper controls. Live notes follow the selected track. If a take is open, the track recording or overdubbing receives them even if you select another track. Each track transmits on its own channel, from track 1 on channel 1 to track 8 on channel 8."}, {"url": "midi-controls.html#program-first-pads", "title": "Program your first pads", "chapter": "MIDI control map", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "In your controller’s editor, create a preset. Set each pad to momentary , choose MIDI note messages and enter the note number directly. On channel 16 , send Note On when you press the pad and Note Off when you release it. Note On with velocity 0 also works as a release. Assign Record to 36, Transport to 39 and Next track to 37. For direct selection, assign two more pads to 60 and 61 for tracks 1 and 2. Save the preset on the controller. In performance mode with the track row set to select, tap the pad assigned to note 61. Track 2 should be highlighted on the OLED. Tap the pad assigned to note 60 to return to track 1. Play a key on the keyboard with its transmit channel set to 1. You should hear the synthesizer play that note. Now select track 2 and set the synthesizer’s receive channel to 2. Play the same key again. The looper’s MIDI thru sends the note on the selected track’s output channel. The supplied DROID patch assigns performance buttons, switches fader banks and receives MIDI feedback. Tapping PLAY/STOP sends note 39 for global transport. Holding it sends note 43 for GROOVE. To move down in the saved-loop picker, assign a spare momentary pad to note 40 on channel 16. For another controller, save the assignments below as a preset or use a MIDI router to translate its messages. The looper uses fixed MIDI assignments and has no on-device MIDI Learn function. Download the DROID patch (.ini) ."}, {"url": "midi-controls.html#button-numbers", "title": "MIDI note assignments for buttons and pads", "chapter": "MIDI control map", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "Send these Note On and Note Off messages from the controller to the looper on channel 16 . The looper recognizes taps and holds. Double-tapping Record sends two press-and-release pairs for note 36. MIDI note Control Use in performance 0–15 Steps 1–16 Restart at that sixteenth-note position in the current bar. 17–24 Bars 1–8 Restart at a bar in the current eight-bar page. 35 NOTELEN / ADD Switch between the two remembered ranges. Double-tap for the full loop. 36 Record Arm or finish a take, or overdub a playing loop. Double-tap to undo, triple-tap to redo. 37 Next track / TRK Select the next track. Hold to open the sets browser when the SD card library is available. 38 Edit Tap to switch the track row between select and mute. Hold for 0.6 seconds and release before 1.5 seconds to enter or cycle editors. Hold for 1.5 seconds to leave editing. 39 Global transport Start or stop the transport when using the internal clock. This is the DROID's Play/Stop tap. 40 Track play/stop Play or stop the selected track. In the saved-loop picker this button moves the cursor down. 43 Groove Dedicated trigger used by the DROID patch's long Play/Stop press. Opens GROOVE on a loop with content or closes the window. 50–57 Slots 1–8 Choose a variation on the selected track or arm an empty slot. 60–67 Tracks 1–8 Select a track, or toggle its mute when the track row is in mute mode."}, {"url": "midi-controls.html#fader-banks", "title": "Fader banks", "chapter": "MIDI control map", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "Each fader sends a MIDI message from the controller to the looper . For a Control Change (CC) assignment, choose the CC number shown and set its value range to 0–127. For Pitch Bend, use the full 14-bit range. Depending on the controller editor, the range reads 0–16383 with centre 8192, or −8192 to +8191 with centre 0. Control NOTE_EDIT LOOP_EDIT GROOVE F1 Pitch Bend, ch 16. Select a note or empty sixteenth-note step. Pitch Bend, ch 16. Move the loop start. CC 20, ch 16. Groove A template. F2 Pitch Bend, ch 14. Adjust the note’s start or end in coarse steps. CC 101, ch 16. Set loop length. Pitch Bend on ch 14 also works. CC 21, ch 16. Groove A amount. F3 CC 2, ch 15. Fine adjustment of the note's start or end. CC 100, ch 16. Fine tempo in 0.1 BPM steps around centre. CC 22, ch 16. Groove B template. F4 CC 3, ch 15. Pitch, or velocity while holding NOTELEN . Pitch Bend, ch 15. Set tempo from 20 to 300 BPM in 1 BPM steps. CC 23, ch 16. Groove B amount. The looper sends Program Change on channel 16 to select a fader bank on the controller. The MIDI program numbers are 0 for the default/loop bank, 1 for note editing and 2 for groove. If your controller requires manual bank selection, recall the matching preset when you switch modes. In GROOVE, the first CC value from each fader establishes its starting position. The next movement adjusts the selected groove parameter. When following external MIDI Clock, adjust the tempo on the device sending the clock."}, {"url": "midi-controls.html#feedback", "title": "MIDI feedback for LEDs and motorized faders", "chapter": "MIDI control map", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "Route MIDI from the looper back to the controller. Set its LEDs to respond to note messages on channel 15 . Note On velocity sets the brightness and Note Off turns the LED off. Assign notes 0–15 to step content, 16–31 to playback position, 39 to transport, 40–47 to bars, 50–57 to slots and 60–67 to tracks. For the bar 1 pad, transmit note 17 on channel 16 when pressed and configure its LED to respond to note 40 on channel 15. For its motorized faders, DROID receives Pitch Bend or CC for position, plus separate note messages to trigger motor movement. A pad controller can use the LED assignments independently. In NOTE_EDIT, DROID uses notes 1 and 2 on channel 15 for both motor triggers and step LEDs. If you build a custom preset, check which controls will respond to these messages. Note-edit motor feedback ."}, {"url": "midi-controls.html#troubleshoot", "title": "When a control does something unexpected", "chapter": "MIDI control map", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "A control pad plays a synth note. Check its transmit channel in a MIDI monitor. Set control pads to channel 16 and your keyboard’s performance zone to one of channels 1–12. One tap mutes a track. Tap Edit once to switch the track row back to selection. Try pad 61 again. A tap behaves like a hold or fires only every other time. Check for one Note On at press and one Note Off at release. Turn off latch/toggle mode and controller-generated repeats. The OLED responds, but there is no sound. Match the synth’s MIDI receive channel to the selected track. Verify that Note On messages arrive at the synth’s MIDI IN or at your DAW’s instrument track. Then check that the instrument’s audio output reaches your headphones or monitors. A DROID LED stays dark or the wrong pad lights. Adjust the LED assignment in midilooper_v1.ini , then reload the patch on DROID. For bar LEDs, check the note numbers under [midiin] and the physical LED outputs under [copy] . Engine button and fader definitions · LED assignments · Input routing · Playback outputs ."}, {"url": "screens.html", "title": "Read the OLED", "chapter": "Screen guide", "group": "Library & controls", "top": true, "body": "Follow the playhead, check which slot you are editing and see when the looper has saved your take."}, {"url": "screens.html#performance-screen", "title": "Read the performance screen", "chapter": "Screen guide", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "Device capture · Performance. Taken from the connected Teensy on 11 November 2026. The transport is stopped on track 2, slot 7. The manual shows the OLED pixels at 4×, with the panel’s yellow-green tint and a black margin around the active display. Track strip, left. When stopped, A means armed, R recording, O overdubbing and P playing. S means the track is stopped on a recorded slot. A dash means the slot is empty. M means muted, including tracks silenced by another track’s solo. The selected track still shows its recording or playback state. When the transport runs, each strip shows MIDI note activity. Piano roll, upper right. Read from left to right to follow the notes in your loop. Higher notes appear higher on the roll. Each note extends from its onset to its release. The playhead marks the current playback position. For loops longer than 16 bars, the piano roll shows one 16-bar page at a time. Status, bottom. Read the four position numbers as bar, beat, sixteenth note and tick. TRACK and SLOT tell you which loop you are viewing. The output channel appears beside them. UNDO shows how many changes you can undo on that slot. During an overdub or note edit, ODUB or EDIT shows the undo count for that session. Loop progress. Brackets mark the section of the loop that repeats. When a new slot is queued, a flashing playhead shows where it will start and a countdown shows how long until it plays."}, {"url": "screens.html#save-display", "title": "Recognize a completed save", "chapter": "Screen guide", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "Look for the four dots above the tempo. Four dim dots mean a save is waiting. One moving bright dot means the looper is writing to the card. All four flash bright when the save finishes. A dimmer flash can indicate a failed save. If you see RO , check that the card was inserted before power-up and that saving is enabled."}, {"url": "screens.html#editing-display", "title": "Read the editing displays", "chapter": "Screen guide", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "In NOTE_EDIT, read the selected note’s start position, pitch, velocity and duration under START, NOTE, VELOCITY and LENGTH. The dotted bracket marks the edit position. LOOP_EDIT shows the loop start, length and tempo. Octave names vary between MIDI instruments. Use the MIDI note number when comparing a displayed pitch with your keyboard or DAW."}, {"url": "screens.html#window-hints", "title": "Use the hints on a window", "chapter": "Screen guide", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "Read the bottom row before pressing a key. [TRK] means Next Track, [EDT] means Edit, [PLY] means Play and [ADD] means NOTELEN. 2* marks a double press. In the GROOVE window the highlighted OFF, A or B shows which timing you hear; changing the other column prepares its next audition."}, {"url": "screens.html#screen-atlas", "title": "Screens by task", "chapter": "Screen guide", "group": "Library & controls", "top": false, "body": "These examples use sample music and library entries. The firmware drew these screens on a computer using the same fonts and layout as the OLED. Select a screen to read how to use its controls. Recording → Playback → Note editing → Loop range → Groove comparison → Set browser → Save a milestone → Load a stage → Get a loop →"}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html", "title": "Troubleshooting", "chapter": "Troubleshooting", "group": "Reference", "top": true, "body": "Use the display and one known MIDI message to narrow down a silent loop, a waiting take or an unexpected button action."}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html#sound", "title": "The track buttons respond, but the synth is silent", "chapter": "Troubleshooting", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "Check the control pads and the keyboard separately. A track pad sends a note on MIDI channel 16 to select a track. The keys you play should transmit on channels 1–12. For a test, select track 1 and play MIDI note 60 on channel 1. Connect your synth to DIN OUT and set it to receive on channel 1, or route USB MIDI channel 1 to a software instrument. Listen to the instrument’s audio output. If you can hear your keyboard but the recorded loop is silent, check the track’s mute and solo settings. Soloing another track can silence the recorded part."}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html#recording", "title": "The record indicator flashes, but the take has not started", "chapter": "Troubleshooting", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "A flashing record indicator means the slot is armed. If the transport is stopped, press global PLAY/STOP . During playback, the take may be waiting for the next bar or the end of the selected track’s loop. Watch the countdown. The looper records or overdubs one track at a time, so finish any other take first."}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html#edit-key", "title": "Tapping a track button mutes it", "chapter": "Troubleshooting", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "A short EDIT press switches the eight track buttons between track selection and mute. Tap it again to return to track selection. To enter NOTE_EDIT on a recorded loop, hold EDIT for about 0.6 seconds and release before 1.5 seconds. A 1.5-second hold exits editing. On an empty slot the shorter hold opens LOOP_EDIT."}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html#missing-notes", "title": "Only part of the loop plays back", "chapter": "Troubleshooting", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "The brackets on the progress bar show the current loop range. Shortening the range can leave recorded notes outside it. In performance or LOOP_EDIT, double-tap NOTELEN to hear the full loop again. For a single missing note, check its velocity in NOTE_EDIT. Raise the velocity above zero to hear it."}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html#storage", "title": "Will my take be there when I power up again?", "chapter": "Troubleshooting", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "Finish the take, leave NOTE_EDIT and wait for the four save dots to flash bright. If you see RO , the looper cannot write to the card. Check the card setup . To keep a version of all eight tracks that you can recall later, choose SAVE SET and wait for confirmation. If the display says BUSY , wait for the current save or load to finish. See Save & load sets for card space and other messages."}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html#loopswitch", "title": "I selected another slot, but the old loop is still playing", "chapter": "Troubleshooting", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "During playback, tap a recorded slot once to queue it for the end of the current loop. Double-tap the new slot to launch it on the next division of the launch grid. The flashing playhead shows where playback will start. With the transport stopped, a single tap selects the slot for the next start."}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html#mapping", "title": "My pad sends the right note number, but the control does nothing", "chapter": "Troubleshooting", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "Check the MIDI channel as well as the note number. Set the pad to send Note On on channel 16 when pressed and Note Off when released. Its LED may receive a different note number on channel 15. For a fader, check which bank is active and whether the assignment uses Control Change or Pitch Bend. Find the exact message in the MIDI control map ."}, {"url": "implementation-notes.html", "title": "Implementation notes", "chapter": "Implementation notes", "group": "Reference", "top": true, "body": "Where the firmware, DROID patch and written controls differ, what you can do on the instrument today, and what could be improved."}, {"url": "implementation-notes.html#binding-owners", "title": "MIDI control assignments are spread across several files", "chapter": "Implementation notes", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "MidiConfig.h defines MIDI channels, note numbers and Control Change (CC) numbers for the legacy firmware. The teensy41-engine target handles incoming MIDI separately. Its Surface::input combines named button assignments with hard-coded channels and CC numbers. EditSurface::value and trigger encode the outgoing MIDI messages for motorized faders. The DROID patch also defines what each physical control transmits and receives. What a player sees. Changing a pad’s note assignment in the legacy configuration leaves the current engine responding to the original note number. If only the engine’s fader input changes, a fader might edit the correct parameter while its motor still listens for feedback on the previous channel or CC number. Suggested next step. Start with one engine binding list covering message type, number, channel, direction and editor bank. Have both the input decoder and feedback writers use it. Check the reference patch and publish the manual's MIDI tables against the same list. Test the existing DROID assignments where MIDI notes 1 and 2 on channel 15 serve as both motor triggers and step LED feedback. Engine build filter · Legacy map · Engine bindings · Input decoder · Motor messages · Reference patch ."}, {"url": "implementation-notes.html#controller-profiles", "title": "Controller presets live outside the device", "chapter": "Implementation notes", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "The firmware matches incoming MIDI messages against fixed assignments. It reserves channels 13–16 for the control surface on all three MIDI inputs. The looper has no MIDI Learn function or editor for controller presets. Assign pads and faders on a programmable controller, or translate their messages with a MIDI router. What a player sees. A sustain pedal sending CC 64 on channel 1 needs a MIDI translator to act as Record . Map pedal down to Note On 36 on channel 16 and pedal up to Note Off 36 on the same channel. This lets the looper distinguish a tap from a hold. Suggested next step. If runtime remapping becomes a product goal, define a small first version around one press/release binding with a default to restore. Specify how a learned control on a musical channel is intercepted, how duplicate assignments are handled, and where the profile persists. Test the learned control during recording with MIDI thru and LED feedback enabled. Channel reservation and message matching · Control versus musical input · Physical input ports ."}, {"url": "implementation-notes.html#base-module", "title": "The four-button base module is incomplete", "chapter": "Implementation notes", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "The intended standalone instrument has four physical buttons and a push encoder. The engine reads the encoder from pins 29 and 30 and its switch from pin 31. You can turn or press it in NOTE_EDIT and GROOVE. The engine does not yet read the four base-module buttons. What a player sees. Use a MIDI controller for Record, transport, editing mode selection and track changes on this build. Suggested next step. Bring one physical Record button through the existing gesture handling first. Exercise arm, recording, undo and redo from both input surfaces and compare feedback, then connect the remaining buttons. Shared action-layer goal · Current hardware support · Encoder input · Encoder actions ."}, {"url": "implementation-notes.html#picker-controls", "title": "The loop picker and the DROID use different Play/Stop messages", "chapter": "Implementation notes", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "The saved-loop picker moves down one row when it receives a tap on note 40, channel 16. With the supplied DROID patch, tapping PLAY/STOP sends note 39 for global transport. Holding it sends note 43 for GROOVE. Pressing the labeled button can start or stop playback while the picker cursor stays in place. Workaround. Assign a spare momentary pad to note 40 on channel 16. In the picker, use NOTELEN for up, that pad for down, REC for left and EDIT for right. Suggested next step. Choose the intended control for Down on the reference controller. Align the patch, picker hint and gesture handler, then check that all eight rows can be reached while playback continues. Picker Down binding · DROID transport note · DROID hold trigger ."}, {"url": "implementation-notes.html#gesture-drift", "title": "Guide entries that disagree with current controls", "chapter": "Implementation notes", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "Entering an editor. The README's first-session walkthrough says to press Edit to open NOTE_EDIT. A tap actually changes the track row between select and mute. Hold Edit for about 0.6 seconds, then release before 1.5 seconds to enter NOTE_EDIT on a filled loop or LOOP_EDIT on an empty slot. A 1.5-second hold exits editing. First-session walkthrough · Edit gesture handler . GROOVE field navigation. The README gesture table assigns “next field” to a short Transport or Track play/stop press in GROOVE. The current handler ignores both. Use Record for the previous field, Edit for the next, or push the encoder for the next. The separate screen-design guide describes that behavior. README gesture table · Window gesture handler · Screen-design guide . Starting with external MIDI Clock. The fixed-memory guide says the transport button does nothing while the external clock source is stopped. If the source keeps transmitting MIDI Clock while stopped, tapping the looper’s transport button arms a local start. Playback begins on the next incoming clock pulse, which the looper treats as a bar line. Fixed-memory guide · External transport handler . Quantizing notes. The README's feature summary lists quantize as unavailable. Its own GROOVE guide describes the shipped STRAIGHT sixteenth-note template. Choose STRAIGHT and raise the amount to pull recorded notes toward the grid. In GROOVE, TRK toggles AUTO Q to apply the groove when an overdub on that loop ends. The dedicated legacy Quantize button is a separate feature. Feature summary · Groove templates · Engine templates · AUTO Q gesture . Suggested next step. Give each gesture one maintained description and link to it from the other guides. Distinguish groove timing, slot launch quantization and the retired legacy control in the feature summary."}, {"url": "implementation-notes.html#library-history", "title": "Milestones and recovery checkpoints omit older card-only undo steps", "chapter": "Implementation notes", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "The storage guide describes a milestone as containing each slot's “full undo history.” The working store can keep older steps on the SD card after they leave memory. The library writer takes its milestone and checkpoint snapshots from History::chain , which walks only the entries in memory. A set load clears both the in-memory history and the slot's older card-only history before installing the snapshot. What a player sees. A saved milestone includes the loops and the undo steps that were in memory at the time of the save. After loading it, you may see fewer undo steps for a loop. To return to the set you were playing before the load, send !ENGINE return over USB serial. There is no Return row in the sets browser. Its recovery checkpoint has the same undo limit. To recall a particular arrangement later, save it as its own milestone while it is current. Suggested next step. Specify whether library saves must preserve card-only history. Add a round-trip check with a known card-only step before promising full-history saves. Keep the workspace and milestone guarantees explicit in the storage guide. Storage guide wording · Snapshot capture · In-memory chain traversal · Clearing cold states during a set replacement · Browser navigation · Serial checkpoint return ."}, {"url": "implementation-notes.html#routing-options", "title": "Track output channels are assigned at startup", "chapter": "Implementation notes", "group": "Reference", "top": false, "body": "The output layer can set a MIDI transmit channel per track. At startup, the firmware assigns track 1 to channel 1 through track 8 to channel 8. The looper sends recorded MIDI events through DIN MIDI OUT and the computer-facing USB port. On the USB host port it transmits MIDI feedback to the control surface. What a player sees. Recording a keyboard on input channel 10 into track 1 produces playback on MIDI channel 1. To play a synth set to receive only on channel 10, change its receive channel or use a MIDI router to remap the track’s output to channel 10. Suggested next step. If user-selectable routing is wanted, expose the existing channel setting and decide whether it belongs to a set or the device. Test playback, live thru and note releases across a routing change before adding a setup screen. Per-track startup assignment · Output channel setting · Port delivery ."}];
